# The campaign machine's parameters (design/CAMPAIGN.md section 2). ffi/campaign.sh
# sources this file; edit it to run the campaign on another machine. Values already
# set in the environment win.
#
# Reference machine (owner, 2026-09-26): Intel Core i9-7900X, 10 cores / 20 threads,
# one socket. Linux usually numbers the first thread of each core 0-9 and the SMT
# siblings 10-19; campaign.sh checks the sibling layout from sysfs, so a different
# numbering is caught, not silently accepted.
#
# CAMPAIGN req 4 as amended by the owner 2026-09-29 (D8): each set is 4 cores WITH BOTH
# SMT threads, and every pool (server, host runtimes, core runtime, grpc-core) is sized to
# the set's 8 threads (D14, 2026-10-03).
#
#   cores 1-4  CLIENT  CPUs 1-4,11-14  (the measured process)
#   cores 5-8  SERVER  CPUs 5-8,15-18  (the RPC server)
#   cores 0,9  OS      CPUs 0,9,10,19  (everything else, the runner included)

AK_MACHINE_NAME="${AK_MACHINE_NAME:-i9-7900X}"
AK_CPU_CLIENT="${AK_CPU_CLIENT:-1-4,11-14}"
AK_CPU_SERVER="${AK_CPU_SERVER:-5-8,15-18}"
# CAMPAIGN req 4 (R-H34, D8): set sizes are fixed for the whole campaign, in CPUs (threads);
# every pool is sized to it (D14).
AK_SET_SIZE="${AK_SET_SIZE:-8}"
AK_WORKERS="${AK_WORKERS:-$AK_SET_SIZE}"
